WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.329 --> 00:00:03.110
INTRO:
We all know Kraft-Heinz as the legacy food

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.060
brand in supermarkets.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.750
From their various condiments to their cheese
and ready made meals, Kraft-Heinz is one of

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.960
the biggest names in food.

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:17.180
Yet, despite their dominant status amongst
the grocery aisles, Kraft-Heinz isn’t actually

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.840
doing that well.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:23.760
Their last few quarters have been hit or miss
in terms of profit, they were recently exposed

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:29.269
for an accounting scandal, and their stock
is down over 50% from their 2017 highs.

00:00:29.269 --> 00:00:34.040
You would think that the top investors would
stay miles away from this company, but ironically,

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:39.290
one of the biggest investors in Kraft-Heinz
is Warren Buffett.

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:44.950
As you would guess, Kraft-Heinz has been one
of Buffett's worst investments of all time.

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:49.910
Buffett has admitted that he did overpay for
the Kraft portion of the business, but he

00:00:49.910 --> 00:00:58.330
has no plans of selling.

00:00:58.330 --> 00:01:04.010
It should be noted though that Buffett owns
26% of Kraft-Heinz, so he really has no easy

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:07.290
way out in terms of selling that large of
a stake.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:11.860
So, his decision to hold may have more to
do with a lack of liquidity than an optimistic

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:13.760
outlook for Kraft-Heinz.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:18.530
This brings up the question, what even happened
to Kraft-Heinz and can they actually recover

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:21.010
like Buffett is hoping?

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:26.950
THE COMING OF KRAFT-HEINZ:
Taking a look back at the history of Kraft-Heinz,

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:32.250
the company’s story actually dates back
over a 100 years to 1876 to a man named Henry

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:33.680
John Heinz.

00:01:33.680 --> 00:01:38.090
Henry was born to German immigrants and was
determined to achieve the American Dream.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:43.700
So, at age 33, he began a small food business
with his cousin and brother.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.729
Just like today, Heinz was basically synonymous
with ketchup in the late 1800s given that

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:50.530
this was their first product.

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:54.640
While the company performed well under Henry’s
leadership, it never became a food behemoth

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:55.640
or anything.

00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.570
It wasn’t until Henry’s son, Howard, took
over and the great depression hit that Heinz

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:00.690
really took off.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:05.510
You see, when money was extremely tight, Heinz’s
ready to eat meals and cheap condiments were

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:09.060
an indispensable part of the average American's
diet.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.360
By the time America recovered from the Great
Depression, Heinz had become a staple amongst

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.480
grocery stores, and it was just a matter of
scaling and maintaining their dominance.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.820
In terms of Kraft’s side of the business,
their history also dates back 100 years to

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:24.350
1909.

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:28.970
Like Heinz, Kraft was also a family business
started by a Canadian immigrant.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:33.510
James L. Kraft and his brothers started off
as door to door cheese salesmen.

00:02:33.510 --> 00:02:37.800
Not only were they good salesmen, but they
were also phenomenal cheese producers.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:42.410
In 1916, they figured out a new method to
pasteurize cheese that gave their cheese a

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.400
significantly longer shelf life.

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.450
They went ahead and patented this technique
which gave them an edge over the competition,

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:53.430
but when they received an acquisition offer
that was too good to refuse, they went ahead

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.270
and sold their company to National Dairy Products
Corporation in 1930.

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:02.600
The Kraft brand was mainly just a subsidiary
for the next 39 years until National Dairy

00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:08.099
decided to revive the brand by renaming the
parent company Kraftco corporation in 1969.

00:03:08.099 --> 00:03:13.239
19 years after this, in 1988, General Mills
would acquire the Kraft Brand and they would

00:03:13.239 --> 00:03:15.700
hold onto it for the next 19 more years.

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:20.459
It wasn’t till 2007 that General Mills started
to sell off Kraft on the public markets which

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:23.290
finally made it an independent company once
again.

00:03:23.290 --> 00:03:29.270
8 years later, in 2015, the boards of both
Kraft and Heinz approved a merger in an attempt

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:30.750
to create a food giant.

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:37.700
But, they ended up creating a food disaster,
so what went wrong?

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:41.910
GENERIC BRANDS:
Likely one of the biggest factors holding

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:47.540
back Kraft-Heinz is the boom of super retailers
like Walmart, Sam's Club, and Costco.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.890
As these guys grow larger, they have started
to directly compete with suppliers.

00:03:51.890 --> 00:03:57.959
For example, Walmart has Great Value, Sam’s
Club has Member’s Mark, and Costco has Kirkland.

00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:02.460
If you’re not familiar with these brands,
they’re comparable to generic brand medicine.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.780
They basically go out and copy all of the
most popular products within their stores.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.150
And, given that such retailers have insane
economies of scale and in house production

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.850
capacity, they can usually offer the same
products for substantially less.

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:20.189
For instance, here’s the official Oreos
and Great Value’s Oreos side by side.

00:04:20.189 --> 00:04:24.689
While these are basically the same product,
Great Value’s offerings are almost half

00:04:24.689 --> 00:04:25.780
the cost.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:33.330
A 19.1 oz packet of Oreos clocks in at $3.98
while a 19.1 oz packet of Great Value Oreos

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:34.330
costs $2.08.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:39.590
Now of course, many people including myself
will always just go with the legacy brand,

00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:43.080
but a good portion of people won’t really
care.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.680
Not only does this drive revenue away from
legacy food brands, but it forces these brands

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:51.219
to be more competitive with price which shrinks
their profit margins.

00:04:51.219 --> 00:04:56.479
Now, this is great news for consumers, but
for name brands who already have meager profit

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:00.629
margins, such competition can easily push
them into unprofitability.

00:05:00.629 --> 00:05:05.319
Fortunately, Kraft-Heinz themselves have pretty
large operations meaning that they can put

00:05:05.319 --> 00:05:07.409
up a good fight against generic brands.

00:05:07.409 --> 00:05:12.009
But, with that being said, such competition
has shrunk their net margins down to the single

00:05:12.009 --> 00:05:13.009
digits.

00:05:13.009 --> 00:05:17.069
Aside from diminishing margins, the truth
is that Kraft-Heinz doesn’t actually have

00:05:17.069 --> 00:05:19.409
that much room to grow.

00:05:19.409 --> 00:05:23.609
While the company has made several acquisitions
to try to continue growth, this hasn’t been

00:05:23.609 --> 00:05:25.419
exactly successful.

00:05:25.419 --> 00:05:29.089
At the end of the day, Kraft and Heinz are
still only known for the two products that

00:05:29.089 --> 00:05:31.680
they were founded on cheese and ketchup.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.600
And the problem is that they’ve basically
reached market saturation within these sectors.

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:41.159
After all, their products are already dirt
cheap and available everywhere in the US,

00:05:41.159 --> 00:05:44.659
so anyone who wants these products can easily
get them.

00:05:44.659 --> 00:05:47.870
Considering this, I don’t think you’d
be surprised to hear that their revenue has

00:05:47.870 --> 00:05:52.159
flatlined at $25 to $26 billion for the past
6 years.

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:55.400
Their revenue also has basically 0 volatility.

00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:00.839
Their best and worst 12 month periods within
the past 6 years are within 7% of each other.

00:06:00.839 --> 00:06:06.319
And while this may be great for stability,
it’s absolutely garbage for growth.

00:06:06.319 --> 00:06:11.849
HEALTHY TRENDS:
Now, you might be thinking, with the introduction

00:06:11.849 --> 00:06:16.659
of in house generic brands, it’s basically
impossible to break into the food industry.

00:06:16.659 --> 00:06:21.250
And while this is partially true given that
the food industry is extremely brutal and

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:26.800
cutthroat, one sector of the industry has
been growing rapidly, and that’s healthier

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:27.800
options.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:30.839
Usually within any market, you have your bougie
sector.

00:06:30.839 --> 00:06:33.340
With cars, you have Mercedes and Audi.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:36.470
With smartphones, you have Apple and Samsung.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.259
And with food, you have organic options and
Whole Foods.

00:06:39.259 --> 00:06:43.909
While this portion of the market is substantially
more profitable, the size of this market is

00:06:43.909 --> 00:06:45.740
generally much smaller.

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:50.040
If you want to grow the biggest business possible,
it’s usually better to target the rest of

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:54.529
the market, and that’s exactly what Kraft
and Heinz have both been doing for over a

00:06:54.529 --> 00:06:55.909
100 years.

00:06:55.909 --> 00:07:00.500
But the problem is that this strategy only
works up until a certain point.

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.399
Once you’re under a price point which consumers
deem fair, they start to make their buying

00:07:04.399 --> 00:07:07.069
decision based on other factors like health.

00:07:07.069 --> 00:07:11.249
Now, this isn’t to say that the average
person is all of sudden strictly buying organic

00:07:11.249 --> 00:07:12.869
foods from Whole Foods.

00:07:12.869 --> 00:07:17.539
But, the average person has trended towards
buying foods with less preservatives, less

00:07:17.539 --> 00:07:20.050
processing, and more natural ingredients.

00:07:20.050 --> 00:07:23.740
And Kraft-Heinz’s offerings are basically
the antithesis to this trend.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:27.899
In fact, if anything, their products have
historically become more processed as they

00:07:27.899 --> 00:07:30.899
try to keep up with the prices of generic
brands.

00:07:30.899 --> 00:07:34.619
Over the past few years, they have shifted
their focus onto the health trend.

00:07:34.619 --> 00:07:38.969
For example, their newer mac and cheese boxes
proudly state that they have no artificial

00:07:38.969 --> 00:07:42.629
preservatives, no artificial flavors, and
no artificial dyes.

00:07:42.629 --> 00:07:45.899
However, this hasn’t been all that successful.

00:07:45.899 --> 00:07:50.150
First of all, Kraft-Heinz was late to this
trend which gave consumers ample time to find

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:51.150
alternatives.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:55.659
And secondly, Kraft-Heinz can’t just change
their association with processed foods which

00:07:55.659 --> 00:07:59.940
has been ingrained in consumers’ minds for
literally a century with a simple marketing

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:00.999
campaign.

00:08:00.999 --> 00:08:04.749
Likely the best option for Kraft-Heinz was
acquiring up and coming brands that aren’t

00:08:04.749 --> 00:08:10.879
held back by this association, but they’ve
screwed this up pretty badly as well.

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:15.389
INTERNAL AFFAIRS:
The first acquisition that didn’t particularly

00:08:15.389 --> 00:08:19.610
help the company was the merger of Kraft and
Heinz itself.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:24.089
Not only did Heinz likely overpay for Kraft
like Warren Buffett suggested, but there was

00:08:24.089 --> 00:08:27.029
really no reason to complete the merger in
the first place.

00:08:27.029 --> 00:08:31.959
The idea was that Kraft and Heinz could share
manufacturing facilities and marketing efforts

00:08:31.959 --> 00:08:35.350
in order to lower costs and reach an even
larger audience.

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:40.490
But as we just discussed, lower prices aren’t
exactly what consumers even want.

00:08:40.490 --> 00:08:45.600
So, all this merger did was lock in Kraft-Heinz
on a cost reduction journey that didn’t

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:46.950
need to take place.

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:51.510
And to make things worse, given how interconnected
the two companies are now, it’s even harder

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:55.130
for them to switch paths compared to if they
were just independent companies.

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:59.960
Also, it’s not just me who thinks that this
acquisition was not the best.

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:03.830
Kraft-Heinz themselves admit that this was
a poor merger which is evident in how they

00:09:03.830 --> 00:09:07.510
wrote off Kraft and Oscar Mayer as a $15 billion
loss.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:13.030
This write off was followed by a dividend
reduction of 36%, And just when you think

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:17.200
things can’t get worse, Kraft also announced
that they were under investigation by the

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:19.470
SEC for their accounting practices.

00:09:19.470 --> 00:09:24.210
Unfortunately, this investigation would result
in evidence that Kraft-Heinz was indeed engaging

00:09:24.210 --> 00:09:26.030
in accounting misconduct.

00:09:26.030 --> 00:09:32.440
Between 2015 and 2018, Kraft had recognized
unearned discounts from suppliers and maintained

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:35.020
false and misleading supplier contracts.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:39.220
This allowed them to understate their expenses
and overstate their profit.

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:43.940
By the end of the investigation, Kraft had
to correct a total of $208 million worth of

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:48.340
bogus cost savings and they were fined a total
of $62 million.

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:52.490
During such tough times, you would hope that
the leadership at the company was strong and

00:09:52.490 --> 00:09:53.490
had a game plan.

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.010
But this wasn’t the case either.

00:09:56.010 --> 00:10:01.910
In April of 2019, the CEO, Bernardo Hees left
the company at the peak of their troubles.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:06.190
It should also be mentioned that his time
as CEO wasn’t that great either.

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:11.610
His legacy at Kraft is literally firing thousands
of employees in an effort to cut costs.

00:10:11.610 --> 00:10:16.680
So, clearly, Kraft–Heinz hasn’t been doing
that great internally or externally for several

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:19.110
years.

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:23.710
BAGHOLDING:
Despite all these shortfalls, Warren Buffett

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:28.070
still believes that both Kraft and Heinz are
wonderful businesses, and that their main

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:30.890
mistake was simply overpaying for Kraft.

00:10:30.890 --> 00:10:35.010
With that being said though, the only reason
he still has a position in the company is

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:37.610
because it’s not easy to get out.

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:41.630
Buffett said quote, “We can't as a practical
matter move around tens of billions of dollars

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:43.010
that easily.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:47.650
But beyond that I mean, if we're working with
a million dollars or $10 million, would I

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:49.180
have a position in it?

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:50.180
No.”

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:53.930
And I think this tells us everything we need
to know about what Buffett really thinks about

00:10:53.930 --> 00:10:55.280
the future of the company.

00:10:55.280 --> 00:10:59.850
He wouldn’t invest in it right now, but
since he already has a position, he’s hoping

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.040
to see it through which is quite possible.

00:11:02.040 --> 00:11:06.420
It’s been some time since the merger happened
and the pandemic started which has allowed

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.320
Kraft-Heinz to regain profitability.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:12.520
Their profits are still low compared to what
they used to be which is leading to a high

00:11:12.520 --> 00:11:14.320
PE ratio of 67.

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:19.150
But, as their profits continue to recover,
this number should only go down.

00:11:19.150 --> 00:11:24.010
Also, Kraft-Heinz may be able to bump up their
dividend again which could attract a lot more

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:25.010
interest into the stock.

00:11:25.010 --> 00:11:29.380
All of these factors could help Kraft-Heinz
stock return to their all time highs and potentially

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:30.650
even beat their highs.

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:35.270
But, given all of the underlying issues that
we’ve discussed so far, I think Kraft-Heinz

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:38.550
will have a difficult time growing much further
than that.

00:11:38.550 --> 00:11:40.330
But that’s just what I think.

00:11:40.330 --> 00:11:43.400
Why do you guys think Kraft-Heinz is performing
so badly?

